You can look at it this way:

        ro da pe le ka ke'a klama le zarci
        Every x associated with the property of going to the store.

        ro da poi ke'a ckaji le ka ke'a klama le zarci
        Every x such that it has the property of going to the store.

        ro da poi ke'a klama le zarci
        Every x such that it goes to the store.

{poi} simply says that you are restricting x to those that have a certain
property, and you need ke'a as the dummy argument for the property.
{ke'a} is always a dummy argument, in properties by themselves or in
the restricting property of {poi}, or in the by-the-way property of {noi}.


